"""Body-text rewrites for Lecture 25 (scaffolding/RULES.md). Figures, captions and videos are untouched."""

body = {
    2: [
        "**Chemoreception** is the detection of chemicals by sensory cells. Clawed lobsters, Homarus americanus, use dissolved food odors to orient while walking across the seabed. Moore, Scholz and Atema recorded paths toward mussel extract and paths during raw-seawater delivery. The chemical stimulus was the main difference between those two conditions. Both conditions shared the same arena and flow.",
        "Odor-search paths had pauses, sideways movement and backward steps, yet they still progressed toward the source. Chemical stimulation changed locomotion rather than simply speeding it up or slowing it. Faster walking did not signal better searching, since odor trials were slower. Mean speed was 5.94 cm/s during odor trials and 10.81 cm/s in controls.",
        "The detailed analysis used motivated, successful approaches, so the recorded paths describe lobsters that reached the source. Individual heading corrections in these paths show how **odor input steers each step** of the search while the animal keeps walking. A source-search path is the sequence of positions the animal occupies, and its length, direction and speed are separate variables.",
    ],
    3: [
        "A **flume** is a channel that supplies controlled flowing water. Moore and colleagues tested lobsters in a 250 × 90 × 20 cm arena with a steady current of 1.0 cm/s. This setup allowed odor-guided walking to be studied under realistic flow, with the animal moving across the floor of the channel toward the source.",
        "Mussel extract is a mixture of food chemicals rather than one identified ligand, so the animal receives many compounds together. Once released, the extract was carried downstream by the current, and the odor reaching the lobster depended on how the flow moved it through the arena. Its release point lay at a fixed height within the arena.",
        "Behavioral differences were attributed to the food extract itself rather than to the current or a delivery tube alone. Because the flow was held steady across trials, the chemical stimulus was the factor that varied between conditions in each session. Each trial set odor exposure beside a control in the same arena.",
    ],
    4: [
        "**Heading angle** is the direction of the lobster's body relative to upstream. Moore and colleagues grouped recorded behavior by distance from the source, which revealed how the approach changes as the lobster nears the odor. Speed and heading were analyzed as separate measures across these distance bins, giving a view of the whole approach.",
        "From 240 to 110 cm away, mean speed increased while heading decreased sharply, so the lobster's body pointed more nearly upstream as it approached. Between 110 and 40 cm, speed held near a steady value. Within the final 40 cm, speed fell and heading increased toward the source. Speed changes were tied to distance rather than to elapsed time.",
        "Successful searching moves through distinct phases: fast, nearly straight travel far from the source, then slower movement with larger heading changes close to it. Distance changes plume structure and concentration together, so each phase matches a changing set of sensory conditions near the source. This sequence describes lobsters that reached the source, not an average over every animal.",
    ],
    5: [
        "**Turning angle** is the change in movement direction between successive path segments. Moore and colleagues compared heading and turn distributions across odor-search trials, testing whether a stereotyped alternating search program appears in the recorded paths. Such a program would produce consistently organized counterturns from one trial to the next. Each recorded path was scored for its heading and turn sequence.",
        "Headings clustered near 0 to 10 degrees, and turns clustered near 10 to 20 degrees. Individual lobsters followed variable paths across repeated trials, producing an upstream bias without a characteristic path repeated at fixed intervals, even within a single animal. The distributions summarize many turns, so one lobster's sequence can still differ markedly from the average.",
        "A simple invariant counterturn account is weakened by this variability in the recorded paths. Bilateral antennular input and water-flow information are candidate sources of the directional corrections, and sensory feedback could modify an internally generated movement sequence on each trial. The paths were recorded from individual animals, so variation within each lobster is directly visible.",
    ],
    6: [
        "**Leg raking** is a repeated movement that brings walking-leg sensory surfaces into contact with the local environment. Moore and colleagues measured raking at several distances from the odor source. The question was whether close-range searching involves more than antennular sampling alone. Raking events were counted in fixed five-second windows at each distance.",
        "Raking was concentrated near the source and was nearly absent far from it. The rate fell steadily as lobsters moved away from the odor source, so most of the recorded raking occurred in the final stretch of each approach, where the animal was closest to the food. Recorded raking events were rarest beyond 22 cm.",
        "The change supports a recruitment of near-substrate sampling during final localization. The authors interpreted it as a switch from antennular guidance to leg-based guidance, while contact exploration or feeding preparation could also raise the raking rate. The paths shown in the figure are repeated source-search trajectories from individual lobsters, which provide spatial context for the raking counts.",
    ],
    7: [
        "The Caribbean spiny lobster, **Panulirus argus**, differs from the clawed lobster in its antennules. These carry aesthetascs, specialized olfactory hairs, together with non-aesthetasc sensilla containing other chemical and mechanical sensory neurons. Both populations offer potentially overlapping information about food odor in the water, so each could guide the search. Both populations are present on each antennule.",
        "Horner, Weissburg and Derby tested animals of 48 to 74 mm carapace length in a 5,000-L flume with flow near 5 cm/s. Shrimp extract at two concentrations supplied high- and low-odor treatments, while seawater served as the chemical control for the same flow conditions. Carapace length defines body size in these crustaceans, and the animals spanned a wide size range.",
        "Selective manipulations targeted each sensory population on both antennules. This design tested whether the remaining pathway could still guide the animal to a food source under realistic flow, rather than under a simplified stimulus that ignores the water movement. Results from the two pathways are compared to determine the contribution of each one.",
    ],
    8: [
        "Horner and colleagues used several interventions because sensory hairs contain different neuron types. Shaving removed nearly all aesthetascs, while removing and sealing non-aesthetasc sensilla targeted the other pathway. Each treatment was chosen to affect one sensory population, and separate controls checked each manipulation. High and low shrimp concentrations were tested against seawater.",
        "A 15-min distilled-water treatment temporarily impaired antennular chemosensitivity, and its effect was reversible within about 24 h. The source sat 2 m upstream of release, and animals were offered shrimp afterward to confirm that their feeding motivation was intact. Feeding after the trial also indicated whether the animals stayed motivated to search.",
        "Some manipulations also affected mechanoreceptors, so a failure to orient can reflect more than chemical loss. The distilled-water treatment is a **nonselective impairment** rather than a molecular receptor knockout, which changes how its effects should be read. Mechanoreceptor loss is therefore a second, nonchemical cause of disorientation in the flume. Both effects matter when a treated lobster fails to orient.",
    ],
    9: [
        "Horner and colleagues measured the proportion of lobsters reaching the odor source after **selective bilateral sensory manipulation**. Intact animals found the high-odor source in most trials and the low-odor source less often. Seawater sources were never located, which set the chemical baseline for the whole experiment. Each success rate is a proportion of trials in which the source was located.",
        "After aesthetasc removal, success stayed high for high odor and for low odor. Removing non-aesthetasc sensilla also preserved source finding, although success fell for the weaker odor, which shows that each pathway carried useful chemical information for the search. Non-aesthetasc removal matched intact success for the weaker odor. Results are reported for each odor separately.",
        "Eliminating antennular chemoreceptor input broadly produced very poor localization across the tested conditions. Either retained antennular chemical pathway can support this food-search task on its own in the flume, as the success rates show. Each retained pathway alone supported that task in the flume. Those results depend on the flume setup used.",
    ],
    10: [
        "**Net-to-gross displacement ratio**, or NGDR, is straight-line displacement divided by total path length, so values closer to one indicate straighter routes. Horner and colleagues measured this variable separately from eventual success, together with arrival time, speed and heading of each animal. Lower NGDR values indicate paths with more turning and side-to-side movement.",
        "At high odor concentration, NGDR was lower after either sensory manipulation than in untreated controls. Arrival times stayed short across the treatment groups, which shows that lobsters still reached the source at a similar pace even though their routes became more winding. Mean arrival times were at most 96 s for high odor and 176 s for low odor.",
        "Remaining sensory pathways can preserve arrival while losing efficient progress. Arrival and path straightness are different properties, since a lobster can succeed after walking farther and correcting its direction more often along the way to the food. Route efficiency and arrival time are separate outcomes of the same search, and each can change on its own.",
    ],
    11: [
        "An **odor plume** is the distribution of odor-bearing water transported downstream from a source. Reidenbach and Koehl visualized plumes with planar laser-induced fluorescence, or PLIF, in which a laser sheet excites dissolved dye and camera intensity estimates local concentration in the illuminated plane. The laser-sheet approach records a single plane at a time.",
        "Their flume produced fine odor filaments separated by cleaner water. Images recorded at high frame rates resolved changes over very short intervals, and the central plume was denser than the sparse encounters found toward its edge, where filaments were thin and spread apart. Images from several lateral positions were compared in this way.",
        "A smooth time-averaged concentration field hides the intermittent encounters and gaps that a moving animal samples. Dye is a transport tracer, so these images describe the physical input reaching a sensory appendage rather than receptor activation itself. Fine filaments of dye appear and vanish at a single point as the plume shifts across the flume.",
    ],
    12: [
        "Reidenbach and Koehl placed virtual sampling lines within measured PLIF images to represent the aesthetasc-bearing portion of an antennule. Lobster and blue-crab geometries were modeled at the same height above the bed, which isolates appendage geometry from receptor sensitivity in the analysis. The crab sampler measured 2 mm in length. Both sampler shapes were placed in identical plume images.",
        "Lobster geometry intercepted odor during more flicks and sampled higher peak concentrations than crab geometry. The lobster's longer sampling structure carried more of the plume into its path, even though each flick moved the sampler a short distance through the water. Flicking rate and geometry together set the total odor sampled by each appendage.",
        "Longer sampling structures encounter more filaments, while faster sampling partly compensates for shorter length. These values come from real plume images analyzed under specified sampling assumptions, which fixes how the appendage's motion is represented in the calculation. Sampling assumptions include the flicking rate and the sampler's downstroke speed used in each analysis.",
    ],
    13: [
        "An **odor-free gap** is an interval without an encounter above the chosen detection threshold. Reidenbach and Koehl compared continuous concentration measurements with the concentrations met by virtual flicking antennules, using the same plume records for both sampling schemes in the analysis. A sensor sampling at discrete times can miss filaments that pass between samples.",
        "Almost all gaps in continuously measured concentration were shorter than 500 ms throughout the sampled plume. Flicking antennules met longer gaps, and those gaps grew as downstream distance to the source decreased, so the sampling schedule changed the input statistics. Longer gaps appeared more often in the intermittent record than in the continuous one.",
        "Encounter frequency changes with distance in this physical model, so it reflects plume transport, detection threshold and flicking geometry together. It works as a timing cue rather than a fixed ruler of distance, and its value depends on the sampling schedule of the animal. Each sampling schedule produces its own gap statistics from the same plume.",
    ],
    14: [
        "Near the seabed, transport and mixing differ from conditions several centimeters above it. Reidenbach and Koehl sampled chemistry at antennular height and at walking-leg height, so the two surfaces could be modeled separately within the same downstream plume. Sensory surfaces at different heights receive different chemical input from the same plume. Sampling heights were set at the two levels.",
        "Mean concentrations sampled near the bed increased toward the source, while mean concentrations sampled higher by flicking antennules decreased toward it. Variance was also lower near the bed across the downstream positions measured, which spanned roughly one to two and a half meters. The plume was sampled at several downstream distances from the source.",
        "The same source produces different patterns for antennules and legs. Close-range leg sampling and distant antennular sampling therefore supply different information, which makes the rise in raking near the source a plausible match to the bed-level signal. The behavioral and transport measurements involve different species and experimental arrangements. Their link remains an interpretation.",
    ],
    15: [
        "**Aesthetascs** are thin-walled sensory hairs carrying the outer dendrites of olfactory receptor neurons. On a Panulirus lateral antennular flagellum, they occur in repeated rows bordered by larger guard hairs, forming a structured array rather than a uniformly exposed sensory surface. Guard hairs mark the edges of each row. Each row contains many aesthetascs.",
        "Reidenbach, George and Koehl measured the biological geometry of these hairs before building physical models of them. The hairs sit at an offset angle, so the orientation of the appendage changes how water enters the gaps between neighboring hairs during each movement. Scanning electron micrographs show the rows and guard hairs directly.",
        "Chemical detection requires molecules to reach this sensory interface before membrane receptors can respond. Guard-hair function is tested through flow measurements in the model, rather than inferred from the protective look of the structure alone. Microscopy establishes dimensions and arrangement, which the physical model reproduces at a larger scale in mineral oil.",
    ],
    16: [
        "A **dynamically scaled model** reproduces the balance of fluid forces despite enlarged structures and a different fluid. Reidenbach and colleagues built glass-rod hair arrays in mineral oil and matched the Reynolds number, the ratio of inertial to viscous effects that governs how the fluid moves. Hair arrays were built at enlarged size in the laboratory.",
        "Particle-image velocimetry, or PIV, tracked suspended particles between images to measure fluid velocity around and between the hairs. The investigators varied orientation, speed and guard-hair presence while the rest of the setup stayed fixed, so each change had a single cause. The model was built from measured biological dimensions and flicking motions.",
        "These controlled changes reveal mechanical causes of water exchange that are difficult to measure inside a living hair array. The model contains no sensory neurons and measures no ligand binding, so its results describe the delivery conditions that can influence olfaction. The physical model stands in for a living antennule, with each result tied to the delivery conditions it reproduces.",
    ],
    17: [
        "An **antennular flick** consists of a fast downward movement followed by a slower return. In the scaled model, PIV measured water velocity inside the hair array during the downstroke. The color map reports velocity rather than chemical concentration or neural activity, so it shows the delivery of water alone. Each flick is a separate delivery event.",
        "During the fast movement, water penetrated the aesthetasc gaps and replaced the previously retained sample. Measured velocity profiles were comparatively uniform across the array, showing that the whole hair field exchanged its water together rather than in one local patch. High-speed recordings placed downstroke motion near an aesthetasc Reynolds number of 2, while return motion sat near 0.5.",
        "Mechanical movement of the organ sets when new water reaches the receptors, before any receptor opens. Fresh water entering the gaps delivers new odor molecules to the sensory surface, and the timing of that delivery follows the flick. Its timing is set by the movement of the appendage rather than by the plume alone.",
    ],
    18: [
        "**Leakiness** describes how much water passes through a hair array rather than around it. Reidenbach and colleagues compared measured downstroke and return-stroke flow, finding that the slower return reduced penetration and kept water near the hairs for longer. The downstroke and return were compared as two phases of one flick. Both phases are part of the same movement cycle.",
        "Return motion was about one-quarter of downstroke speed, yet water between the hairs moved about 25 times more slowly. Slowing the appendage alters array permeability far more than it alters the appendage's own movement, which makes the return stroke a sampling phase in its own right. The effect is measured as a difference in flow between the two strokes.",
        "A slow return keeps captured water beside the aesthetasc surfaces, which gives molecules time to move toward the sensory layer before the next downstroke. The retained sample therefore sits in place long enough for chemical contact to occur. Water that moves through the array carries dissolved molecules into the gaps. The effect is physical and happens before any receptor responds.",
    ],
    19: [
        "The shape and orientation of an aesthetasc array determine how incident water moves through it. Reidenbach and colleagues measured the observed zigzag orientation, offset by about 32 degrees, alongside a zero-degree arrangement. They also measured arrays with and without the surrounding guard hairs in place. Both arrangements were tested in the same model flow.",
        "The observed orientation produced relatively uniform water penetration during the flick. Removing the guard hairs changed the spatial velocity profile, which reveals a hydrodynamic contribution beyond any protective role that these hairs might play for the animal. The uniform penetration occurs across the whole array rather than through a few gaps. The profile is measured, not inferred.",
        "The findings link anatomical arrangement to the distribution of a fresh sensory sample across the array. Diffusion, receptor activation and central interpretation determine what follows from that sample, and the model measured none of them directly. Guard hairs change the water entering each array, which links structure to delivery. Those effects are physical rather than neural.",
    ],
    20: [
        "Koehl and colleagues programmed a mechanical lobster to flick real Panulirus antennules within a fluorescent dye plume. The preparation kept the biological hair array while the movement was controlled, and dye from a benthic source stood in for food chemicals carried by the current. The dye marked where the plume's water traveled.",
        "High-speed frames show dye parcels entering the array during the rapid downstroke. During the slower return, dye stayed trapped between the hairs while the surrounding plume kept moving, and the next downstroke shed that sample and captured another from the plume. Parcels of dye were visible as they moved into and out of the array.",
        "The frames show discrete water sampling rather than continuous exposure to ambient concentration. Fluorescent dye tracks transported water and solute, but it records no receptor binding, and the mechanical preparation omits the feedback an intact animal uses to adjust its flicks. A mechanically driven flick is simpler than an animal's own flicking behavior.",
    ],
    21: [
        "Koehl and colleagues measured concentration along the aesthetasc-bearing section of the antennule through successive video frames. Their spatiotemporal map places position on one axis and time on the other, with color showing dye concentration at each point along the appendage. Successive frames trace the same positions as the flicks proceed. The map covers the aesthetasc region.",
        "The spatial pattern changed during rapid downstrokes and stayed comparatively stable during the slower return and stationary intervals. Dye spread along the antennule during acquisition, so the captured pattern was altered from the original plume it came from. The dye pattern changed most during the downstroke, when the appendage moved fastest. The change was visible in consecutive frames.",
        "Sampling both retains and filters information before any neuron responds. A spatially uneven retained sample could drive different sensory endings to different degrees, which makes spatial structure part of the input the animal receives. The retained pattern is a physical product of the flick. The retained pattern shapes what later neurons can encode.",
    ],
    22: [
        "**Ionotropic receptors**, or IRs, are chemosensory proteins related to ionotropic glutamate receptors, a family of ligand-gated membrane channels. Corey and colleagues identified candidate IR sequences in Panulirus olfactory tissue and used in situ hybridization to locate their RNA within sensory cells. Antisense probes detect complementary target RNA, while sense probes supplied negative controls for nonspecific staining.",
        "IR25a and IR93a signals were broad, appearing in most olfactory receptor neurons. IR8a and another candidate showed narrower expression across the sampled cells. Shared components therefore appear in most neurons, while more variable candidates appear in only some cells, a shared core with variable additions. Those two candidates were the most restricted.",
        "These findings support an IR-based molecular architecture in lobster olfaction. Expression data narrow the candidate list, and functional tests identify the ligand of each candidate receptor. Protein and channel recordings then link those RNA patterns to odor function in the cell, and each receptor needs its own separate functional test in situ.",
    ],
    23: [
        "RNA expression shows where a gene is active, but the location of the protein requires its own test. Corey and colleagues used an antibody against IR25a to localize the protein. Cross-sections through distal aesthetasc hairs gave access to the outer dendrites, where transported odorants first approach olfactory neurons. Protein staining answers a separate question.",
        "IR25a immunofluorescence appeared within the dendritic profiles. Sections stained without the primary antibody served as a background control, and an antibody against an Ih-channel protein provided a separate neuronal comparison. Western blots detected a band near the predicted molecular mass of about 100 kDa. Blots and staining used separate samples and methods, which strengthens the identification.",
        "Biochemical detection and dendritic staining together place a candidate receptor component at a plausible transduction site. Antibody specificity, membrane localization and odor-evoked current are separate lines of evidence, each establishing a different property of the channel. Immunolabeling reveals where the protein is present in the tissue, not whether it forms an open channel.",
    ],
    24: [
        "Corey and colleagues loaded sensory-cell clusters with a calcium-sensitive dye and applied different chemical stimuli. Calcium imaging reports changes in intracellular calcium through changing fluorescence, and it records many cells at once rather than one neuron at a time. Each single cell was scored separately for every chemical in the stimulus set.",
        "The stimulus set included food extract, arginine and glutamate at 1 mM for the amino-acid tests. Neighboring cells showed different response profiles across this stimulus set, and a heat map displays that heterogeneity cell by cell rather than as one shared cluster response. Individual cells responded to some stimuli and not others. Those contrasts appeared within a single cluster.",
        "The responses establish chemical selectivity at the cellular level, even though common IR components are broadly expressed. Expression and physiology together motivate receptor tests, which identify the ligand that each receptor recognizes in the cell. Fluorescence amplitude is an indirect indicator of activity rather than a direct current measure. Expression patterns motivate the receptor experiments that follow.",
    ],
    25: [
        "Whole-organ sequencing combines RNA from sensory neurons, support cells and other tissue. Kozma and colleagues instead collected individual fluorescently identified olfactory sensory neurons for single-cell transcriptomics, sequencing the expressed RNA of each cell separately from the others. Fluorescent identification ensured that each cell was an olfactory sensory neuron. Support cells were excluded from each sample.",
        "All seven analyzed single-cell transcriptomes contained IR25a and IR93a. They also contained variable tuning-IR transcripts, while IR8a and IR76b were detected in none of those cells, so the shared components appeared in every sampled neuron. Seven cells were analyzed, a small sample of the full population. Each cell contributed its complete transcript profile.",
        "The result supports shared receptor components with cell-specific combinations of additional candidates. Transcript detection shows gene expression in each cell, while protein abundance and channel assembly need measurements in the same preparation. Each cell therefore contributes its own list of expressed genes in this sample. The transcript list alone says nothing about protein levels.",
    ],
    26: [
        "**Tuning IRs** are variable receptor candidates proposed to contribute ligand selectivity alongside widely expressed components. Kozma and colleagues compared their abundance across single olfactory sensory neurons, with receptor identity shown by row and each cellular sample by column. The heat map uses color to show how strongly each transcript is expressed in each neuron.",
        "Each analyzed neuron held 9 to 53 tuning-IR transcripts, often with just a few at high levels. This pattern breaks the simple rule that each neuron expresses one ligand-selective receptor. Levels varied sharply from one receptor to the next within each cell. Low-abundance transcripts could also reflect technical sampling rather than biology.",
        "Shared coreceptors coexist with molecular diversity between cells. The transcript data constrain candidate combinations, while receptor composition, surface delivery and ligand specificity require protein-localization and functional experiments. The data show diversity among individual neurons rather than a uniform pattern across the sampled cells. Each candidate combination in each cell needs its own functional test.",
    ],
    27: [
        "Kozma and colleagues also examined **G-protein-coupled receptors**, or GPCRs, and **transient receptor potential channels**, or TRP channels, in sensory neurons. GPCRs regulate intracellular signaling, whereas TRP proteins form ion channels with diverse sensory and cellular functions across many tissues. Both receptor families were studied in the same neurons. Their transcripts were quantified in each neuron.",
        "Individual cells expressed 12 to 17 GPCR candidates and overlapping sets of TRP-channel genes. The published summary includes common proteins and additional variable candidates, which together outline potential molecular machinery alongside the IR family in these neurons. The summary distinguishes shared proteins from variable ones. Counts varied from cell to cell within the sample.",
        "A GPCR in an olfactory neuron may receive modulatory input rather than bind a food odor. Connecting these genes to mechanism requires ligand responses, channel properties and perturbations in identified cells. A TRP transcript identifies a candidate channel that must be tested for the primary receptor current. Each candidate needs its own functional test.",
    ],
    28: [
        "Bobkov and colleagues recorded Panulirus sensory neurons in a semi-intact antennular preparation. Odor reached the outer dendrites while the cell bodies stayed accessible to electrodes and imaging, and separate fluid compartments allowed repeated inputs to the sensory surface. Calibrated pulses could be repeated on the same sensory surface across each trial series.",
        "A fluorescent tracer characterized stimulus timing, while calcium imaging and loose-patch recordings measured the responses. Food extract was tested at concentrations up to 0.5 mg/mL, and increasing pulse duration increased the effective delivered stimulus during each trial. Calcium signals and loose-patch spikes were recorded from the same cells. The tracer confirmed when each pulse reached the sensory surface.",
        "These calibrations establish when chemical input arrived relative to cellular activity. Pulse duration, onset kinetics and effective dose covary, so concentration dependence must be read from the actual delivery waveform rather than the source concentration alone. Waveform calibration makes the stimulus at the sensory surface explicit for every recorded response. Each response is matched to the waveform that produced it.",
    ],
    29: [
        "Bobkov and colleagues found tonic excitation, phasic-tonic excitation and inhibition among olfactory receptor neurons exposed to food extract. Tonic responses persist during stimulation, while phasic responses emphasize its beginning, and inhibitory responses reduce activity below the unstimulated level. Sensory neurons carry distinct response types. Some excitatory cells responded more briefly than others.",
        "Whole-cell voltage-clamp recordings near -70 to -60 mV measured inward or outward odor-evoked currents alongside firing patterns. Excitatory current could persist or adapt rapidly, while some cells showed inhibitory currents during the same odor exposure. Inward and outward currents were both measured across cells. Those currents were read at a set holding voltage.",
        "Peripheral cells transmit different signs of input, so inhibition is already present in the receptor population. A current's direction depends on holding voltage and ionic driving force rather than on the behavioral meaning of an odor. Inhibitory responses are part of peripheral coding rather than a central product. Tonic and phasic classes differ in their timing.",
    ],
    30: [
        "Calcium-sensitive fluorescence is often used as a proxy for neuronal activity, but it is not an action potential. Bobkov and colleagues recorded spikes and calcium simultaneously in identified lobster sensory neurons, testing how closely the optical signal follows the electrical output of each cell. Fluorescence follows activity with a delay in the recorded cells.",
        "Tonic, phasic-tonic and inhibitory neurons showed corresponding but differently filtered fluorescence changes. Calcium generally changed more slowly than the spike pattern, and the optical peaks lagged the bursts they followed. Spike and calcium traces were compared cell by cell. Each class showed its own optical profile, and each cell served as its own reference.",
        "Calcium supports the identification of response classes, while precise encounter timing requires spike recordings. Dye kinetics, intracellular buffering and calcium removal all intervene between spikes and fluorescence in the recorded cells. Spike recordings resolve individual events within each cell. Each measurement answers a different question. Both readouts were taken from identified neurons.",
    ],
    31: [
        "A **burst** is a cluster of spikes separated from another cluster by a quieter interval. Bobkov and colleagues observed intrinsically bursting olfactory receptor neurons in the absence of presented odor, so spontaneous output already has temporal structure before any stimulation arrives. The bursting pattern provides intrinsic timing separate from the odor input.",
        "Simultaneous recordings showed repeated electrical bursts followed by slower calcium oscillations. Across cells, optical oscillation frequencies ranged from about 0.015 to 0.9 Hz, with an average near 0.16 Hz, so each cell supplied its own intrinsic temporal reference. Lower frequencies reflected slower recurring bursts, and higher ones recurred more quickly. Electrical bursts preceded each calcium cycle.",
        "These observations establish spontaneous peripheral timing rather than a clock shared by the whole antennule. An odor-evoked burst must be separated from a spontaneous burst when judging whether a cell has detected a new encounter. Spontaneous bursts give each cell its own baseline rhythm before odor arrives. Odor arrival modifies that baseline in measurable ways.",
    ],
    32: [
        "Bobkov and colleagues imaged many sensory somata within the same antennular cluster and compared their calcium time series. Correlation measures how strongly two signals vary together, and pairwise comparisons tested whether nearby bursting cells behaved as one synchronized network. Each pair of neighboring cells was then compared across the recording. Its correlation was scored for every pair in the cluster.",
        "Most recorded pairs were weakly correlated and had different oscillatory timing. A small number of highly similar records may reflect overlapping optical sampling, so those pairs are judged with care before any cellular coupling is inferred from them. Most cells in the cluster oscillated independently of one another. Such results argue against shared timing across neighboring somata.",
        "Nearby sensory-cell oscillations were largely independent in this preparation. Neighboring bursting cells can provide diverse temporal signals, which may combine centrally into a more informative population signal than any single cell supplies. Diverse oscillators can carry more information together than any one cell alone. Central combination of their outputs is the proposed mechanism.",
    ],
    33: [
        "A **second messenger** is an intracellular molecule whose concentration changes after signaling begins. Michel and Ache tested cyclic nucleotides in lobster sensory neurons that were excited by food extract but inhibited by proline. Whole-cell recordings were made at a holding voltage of −60 mV, which allowed the outward current to be measured directly.",
        "Proline evoked a small outward current in this cell class. The phosphodiesterase inhibitor IBMX, applied at 1 mM, mimicked that outward response, as did membrane-permeant cyclic-nucleotide analogs. Together, these manipulations connect accumulation of cyclic nucleotides to the conductance seen with proline. Holding the cell at a fixed voltage made the direction of the current measurable.",
        "These manipulations link intracellular cyclic-nucleotide accumulation to a conductance associated with peripheral inhibition. The interpretation builds on an odor-evoked potassium conductance characterized earlier in this receptor-cell system. Pharmacological mimicry identifies a pathway, not the odor-binding receptor that starts it. Their outward current depends on the ionic driving force at the holding voltage.",
    ],
    34: [
        "**Adenylyl cyclase** synthesizes cyclic AMP, or cAMP. Michel and Ache applied forskolin, an activator of this enzyme, to test whether increasing its activity could reproduce the proline-associated inhibition. Saline application served as a control for the mechanical delivery of solution in the same chamber. Forskolin is a pharmacological tool for raising cAMP synthesis.",
        "Forskolin at 50 µM evoked an outward current in proline-inhibited neurons. IBMX enhanced related responses by slowing nucleotide breakdown. Cells excited by food extract but not inhibited by proline showed a different response class, so the effect depends on which neuron class is recorded. Forskolin's effect shows the enzyme can produce the outward response when activated.",
        "These results support cyclic-nucleotide participation in inhibitory transduction. Modern expression studies and earlier biochemical findings point to one shared pathway, although the receptor that starts it is a separate question for the field. Those findings are compatible with modern IR expression data. The finding ties the proline response to a specific enzyme class.",
    ],
    35: [
        "Boekhoff and colleagues isolated membranes enriched from the outer dendrites of Panulirus olfactory hairs. A quench-flow assay mixed membrane material with the stimulus and stopped the reaction rapidly, so biochemical changes over milliseconds could be measured apart from spike output. Enrichment concentrates the outer-dendrite material before the assay. The assay then measured messenger levels at each time point.",
        "Food extract produced transient increases in cAMP and inositol 1,4,5-trisphosphate, or IP3, peaking within about 50 ms. IP3 is a signaling molecule produced from membrane phosphoinositides, so both messengers rose within the same brief window after stimulation. Both messengers were quantified in the same membrane samples. Their rise was transient rather than sustained.",
        "The timing is fast enough to take part in early sensory transduction, well before any behavioral response. The data reflect pooled dendritic material, so it describes odor-associated signaling in aggregate rather than in a single neuron. Measured messenger levels are biochemical quantities, so they are compared across conditions rather than read as spike rates.",
    ],
    36: [
        "Boekhoff and colleagues compared taurine and proline because one ligand can excite a lobster sensory neuron while another inhibits it. At 1 µM, taurine preferentially elevated IP3 and proline preferentially elevated cAMP in isolated dendritic membranes. Each ligand's preference was assessed at both doses. The ratio of IP3 to cAMP shifted between the two doses.",
        "At the higher dose of 100 µM, both ligands elevated both messengers while retaining their different preferences. The published plots show these distinct low-dose time courses, which differ in peak timing and size. The higher dose raised both messengers above their low-dose levels. The two messengers peaked at different times for each ligand.",
        "Both ligands shifted their messenger ratio as concentration increased, so no single ligand kept a fixed pathway. Pooled membranes report the signal without naming the receptor proteins or cell combinations behind it. The concentration series makes the shift visible for each ligand separately. Taurine and proline both showed this shift. Each ligand's preference appeared at both doses.",
    ],
    37: [
        "Park and colleagues recorded bursting olfactory receptor neurons and quantified the intervals between spontaneous bursts. A quasiperiodic oscillator repeats approximately, with variability rather than a perfectly fixed period, so each cell carries its own rhythm. Spontaneous bursts supplied all of these intervals. No odor was applied during these recordings. Each cell's intervals were summarized separately.",
        "The recorded cells displayed different mean burst intervals and different amounts of within-cell variation. Some repeated over a few seconds, whereas others had substantially longer intervals, and electrical records are set against interval distributions. Within-cell variation was measured for each cell separately. The original records show the electrical traces directly. Within-cell variation differed among the cells recorded.",
        "Such intrinsic activity offers a possible reference against which incoming odor encounters can be registered. An intrinsic period describes the recorded cell under these conditions, which makes it a property of the cell rather than of the plume. A population of such cells could encode when encounters occurred. Each cell adds its own interval to that code.",
    ],
    38: [
        "Phase describes where a neuron lies within its recurring burst cycle. Park and colleagues delivered comparable odor pulses at different times after the previous spontaneous burst, testing whether the same chemical input has different effects depending on the cell's current state. Burst timing thus defines the cell's current state. Each pulse was judged against the cell's spontaneous firing.",
        "In an example cell, burst probability was about 50% when odor arrived 3 s after the preceding burst and approached 100% at 6 s. The same odor intensity produced different outcomes depending on timing, and recordings also showed changes in electrical activity and burst timing. The example cell shows the effect of timing directly.",
        "The result establishes history-dependent sensory responsiveness. A refractory or low-responsiveness phase can hide an encounter in one cell, while other cells at different phases can still respond to the same odor. Population activity with different periods could carry the timing of encounters. Behavioral use of that information is a further hypothesis. The pattern is a measured relationship between timing and response probability.",
    ],
    39: [
        "Park and colleagues fitted oscillator-response models using experimentally measured properties of bursting neurons. A decoder is an analysis rule that estimates an input from output activity, so the question was whether these response rules could carry temporal information. The decoder reads the pooled output of many fitted cells, not any single cell, in the simulation.",
        "The model estimated the intervals among presented events, and combining cells with different intrinsic periods improved access to the time since a previous encounter. The displayed curves are the authors' published synthetic-population results. Each added cell with a different period contributed its own timing signal. The authors' estimates use simulated intervals rather than recorded animal behavior.",
        "Successful model decoding shows coding feasibility under the model's assumptions. Connecting the proposed code to searching would require selective disruption of bursting and measurement of plume tracking. The model motivates that experiment without replacing it. Such an experiment would link a proposed neural code to its behavioral consequence. Model parameters set the scope of that prediction.",
    ],
    40: [
        "An **afferent neuron** carries sensory input toward the brain. Wachowiak and Cohen electrically stimulated the lobster antennular nerve and recorded voltage-sensitive dye signals in the nerve and olfactory lobe. The anatomy identifies the central region that receives these sensory axons. Its response to repeated shocks was measured in the olfactory lobe. Stimulation used two shocks per trial.",
        "Two 2-ms shocks separated by approximately 300 to 400 ms produced a smaller second response in the olfactory lobe, while the nerve response was less affected. Calcium-free, high-magnesium saline reduced synaptic transmission, which helped separate incoming axonal signals from activity generated by the local network. Nerve responses were recorded simultaneously with the olfactory-lobe signal.",
        "The pattern places suppression beyond peripheral conduction in this tested preparation. Electrical shocks also synchronize afferents differently from natural odor, and population optical signals still average over many synapses. Suppression appears in central neurons rather than in the sensory hairs. The second response was measured relative to the first within each pair.",
    ],
    41: [
        "GABA, gamma-aminobutyric acid, and histamine can act as neurotransmitters. Wachowiak and Cohen applied them while measuring lobster afferent voltage signals under calcium-free conditions, which reduced network transmission and isolated effects on incoming sensory processes. Calcium-free conditions were chosen to reduce ordinary downstream synaptic activity. The recordings measured afferent voltage, not spike output.",
        "At 100 µM, histamine reduced the measured response by about 82% and GABA by about 45%. At 30 µM, histamine still reduced it by about 61%, whereas GABA had little effect. Responses recovered during washout. Histamine inhibited the response at both concentrations tested. The GABA response was largely absent at the lower dose.",
        "The authors proposed shunting inhibition, in which a ligand-gated conductance lowers membrane resistance and reduces terminal spike amplitude or propagation. The data identify dose-dependent inhibitory sensitivities without measuring odor-search behavior. Presynaptic calcium entry in the lobster would test the proposed mechanism directly. Recovery during the washout indicates that the effects were reversible.",
    ],
    42: [
        "A **receptor antagonist** reduces activation of a receptor by its ligand. Wachowiak and Cohen used antagonists to test GABA-sensitive and histamine-sensitive inhibition in lobster olfactory afferents, following control recordings from the same preparation. Their effects were measured as changes in afferent voltage signals. Histamine and GABA were the two transmitters examined in these experiments.",
        "An isolated nerve segment was fairly unaffected by these drugs, which supports a central-terminal locus. The separation of the two actions suggests distinct receptor mechanisms for each transmitter. Exact channel subunits were not identified in these experiments, and natural release during a lobster's food search was never recorded directly in the nerve.",
        "The drugs act as useful but imperfect selective labels, and their selectivity varies with concentration. The findings constrain receptor classes and the place where modulation occurs rather than naming exact channel subunits. Each drug was chosen for one receptor class, and different concentrations tested those classes. These two drugs supplied the pharmacological tests in the same nerve.",
    ],
    43: [
        "**Odor flux** combines chemical concentration with the motion carrying that chemical. Pravin and Reidenbach measured flow and fluorescent concentration in a flume, then built a three-dimensional fluid model constrained by those measurements, which addresses direction information unavailable from concentration alone. Concentration alone indicates where odor is present, not which direction it moves.",
        "The authors' model showed transverse chemical transport toward the plume center on opposite sides of the plume. Useful differences emerged over averaging intervals of about 0.5 s, and the colored traces show modeled transverse flux rather than trajectories. The modeled flux differs on each side of the plume. These differences appeared only after averaging.",
        "Joint chemical and mechanical sensing could provide a directional cue. The study establishes that this cue is physically available, while its biological use remains a hypothesis. The proposed mechanism is kept separate from the established source-search and lesion results. No lobster neuron was shown multiplying velocity by concentration. The model's flux values are presented as model outputs, not biological signals.",
    ],
    44: [
        "**Intermittency** describes the temporal discontinuity of odor. Michaelis and colleagues quantified it as the count of concentration spikes crossing a threshold within a time window, using a turbulent-plume model validated against laboratory fluorescence and velocity measurements. The model reproduces the physical plume rather than an animal's sensory response. Spikes are counted as crossings of the chosen threshold.",
        "The modeled domain was 5 × 0.5 × 0.15 m with mean flow near 0.02 m/s. Intermittency profiles depended on cross-stream position and on the length of the observation window, so the statistic varies across the plume. Spatial profiles differ between the plume center and its edges. Profiles were computed for several observation windows.",
        "At plume edges, bilateral differences in encounter frequency could remain informative when instantaneous concentration was unreliable. These results propose a statistic that complements a mean concentration reading. Physical validation strengthens the plume representation of this statistic. Validation of the sensory threshold and memory window was not part of the model. Bilateral neural comparisons remain a hypothesis for the model.",
    ],
    45: [
        "Michaelis and colleagues compared modeled intermittency estimates at different sampling frequencies and tested simulated search rules. A finite observation window and sample schedule change the estimated chemical statistic, so sampling strategy becomes part of the navigation hypothesis. Their simulated search rules were run at each sampling frequency, and the estimate changed with each one.",
        "Their results support temporal encounter information alongside concentration under specified plume conditions. Earlier work linked flicking to encounter gaps, bursting neurons to recent history, and one antennular pathway to path straightness. Each link came from a separate preparation, so the three kinds of evidence are not yet joined together in one experiment.",
        "Together, the studies connect physical acquisition, peripheral coding and source-directed behavior across several preparations. A successful model search and receptor expression each support the mechanism, and neither alone establishes it. Explicit links between timing and behavior require direct manipulation in live animals, which would test whether the proposed code drives searching in natural plumes.",
    ],
}
